 chris kyle is the prime example of a  dutiful soldier in the film he's  motivated exclusively by a desire to  protect the lives of his teammates he's  willing to put himself in extremely  dangerous situations if it means he can  save lives i'm gonna go clear houses  with marines  and though these experiences cause him  trauma the film ultimately tells us it's  worth it because he saved american lives  your dad he's a hero on the surface this  stance appears apolitical because kyle  is just protecting soldiers in dangerous  situations but did those soldiers have  to be there in the first place  earlier in the film chris kyle's dad  lectures him on the importance of using  violence to protect  others and while he lectures the film  cuts to chris as a child  using violence to protect his brother  and then there are those who've  been blessed with the gift of aggression  and the overpowering need to protect the  flock  this scene clearly serves as an analogy  for chris's role as a sniper in the iraq  war he's just  using violence to protect people in  danger but if you look at this fight as  a metaphor for the iraq war the moral  justification breaks down  was beating up this other kid really the  only option to protect his brother  and did he really have to continue  punching the other kid even after his  brother already escaped  even though the film appears to not take  a stance one way or the other on the  validity of the war the narrow focus on  the individual totally simplifies the  moral greatness of the actual iraq  invasion nowhere along the way doesn't  consider that the best way to support  the troops is to keep them out of harm's  way in the first place and it never  questions whether troops are actually  improving the situation in iraq the film  tries to evoke the same feeling  americans had during world war  ii there was good and evil and we had to  fight on the side of good no matter the  cost  i just want to believe in what we're  doing here well there's evil here we've  seen it  in reality the iraq war has much more in  common with the moral grayness of  vietnam because both conflicts have  since been seriously questions  all across the political spectrum so  really american sniper tries to give a  world war  ii type moral absolutism to a vietnam  war type conflict  and other popular iraq war films also  try redressing the iraq war with a  morally righteous story  zerodark 30 is honest about some  controversial aspects of the war but it  still avoids questioning the war itself  the film justifies the shocking torture  scenes by having the torture result in  crucial information about bin laden's  whereabouts  in reality america discovered midlands  location from surveillance  not torture and torture has been  routinely questioned as an effective  interrogation tactic  just like american sniper this film  creates a scenario where the  justification for violence appears  obvious but only because the film framed  that scenario in a way that overlooks  the broader context of the war  contrast these films with films like  platoon and the deer hunter where the  horrors of war crimes are addressed  head-on and clearly portrayed as a  mistake  or even world war ii films like saving  private ryan where interesting questions  do get raised about the justification of  their mission  because world war ii and vietnam war  films make moral judgments about their  respective conflicts that honesty allows  them to address more interesting  questions about war  in general overall mainstream iraq war  films focus on specialized experts and  their individual experiences in combat  and that focus means that these films  rarely address moral issues about the  iraq war as a whole  not to mention they completely overlook  the iraqi perspective  maybe this is why the genre doesn't have  its apocalypse now if a war film wants  to be truly great it needs to confront  the messy reality of the war that it's  about  although most mainstream iraq war films  avoid the messy realities there are some  films that do address american  involvement in the middle east  in challenging ways what the [ __ ] are  you even doing here  sir i got lost on the way to college sir  jarhead is a 2005 film about infantry  men in the gulf war though it is about a  different war  its time of release setting and subject  matter make it feel comparable to an  iraq war film and unlike most iraq war  films  jarhead does have something insightful  to say about american involvement in the  middle east  in the film swafford's character  satirizes the american fantasy of a  morally justified war effort it feels  almost as if swafford watched american  sniper and got the impression that he  could be a hero too but from the  beginning the film rejects the idea that  he's a morally righteous character doing  the right thing  he was manipulated into joining the  military he's sickly obsessed with  killing  i wanted the pink mist and in the end he  was completely worthless to the war  effort  i never shot my rifle after a grueling  experience in boot camp and months of  waiting in the hot desert  all the soldiers want is to get into  battle but when it finally comes the  enemy retreats almost instantly american  air power easily overpowered the iraqi  army  damn you too [ __ ] slow they already  retreat all right we can shoot a  thousand yards  to go that far in vietnam that'll take a  week more one a year  here it's gonna take about 10 [ __ ]  seconds they then spend the rest of the  film walking through the carnage brought  by a war that moves too fast for them  and when swafford finally gets his  chance to fire his weapon  what the [ __ ] frequency are you on [ __ ]  we got air i'm calling it in like other  iraq war films it focuses on the  experience of the american  soldier but that experience is not one  of duty and honor it's one of  psychological torture the scars they  bring home aren't shown as sacrifices  for our freedom  instead 